<Doc
  title="How to apply it"
  kicker="Chapter 03 — the guide"
  description="A practical guide to applying OTAKU in object-oriented code: discipline, composition, a protected core, silent infrastructure and contracts."
  path="/guide"
>
  <p class="lede">
    The manifesto says what is mandatory. This guide says what it looks like in code
    — one rule at a time, with the violation first, because most people recognize
    their own code faster in the wrong example.
  </p>

  <h2>The method</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Read the rule.</strong> It is one sentence and it is not negotiable.</li>
    <li><strong>Find the violation.</strong> Look at the smallest example that breaks it.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply the smallest fix.</strong> Not a redesign — the smallest change that satisfies the rule.</li>
    <li><strong>Make it impossible to regress.</strong> A linter, an architecture test, a review checklist.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Every lesson ends with an enforcement step. A rule that only lives in a document
    is a preference; a rule that fails the build is a constraint.
  </p>

