  <h2>About the code examples</h2>

  <p>
    Examples are annotated as <strong>PHP</strong>, <strong>C#</strong>,
    <strong>Java</strong> or <strong>C++</strong>. Where the rule is syntactic and the
    four languages disagree only in punctuation, one example is shown with a note.
    Where the rule is structural, all four are shown — because that is exactly where
    people assume their language is an exception.
  </p>

  <p>
    The language tabs are rendered by CSS, not JavaScript. The site ships no client
    runtime at all, which is the same constraint the philosophy asks of your domain.
  </p>

  <h2>Languages covered</h2>

  <p>
    PHP, C#, Java and C++ share the object-oriented vocabulary this philosophy needs:
    classes, interfaces, exceptions, generics (or their approximation) and a package
    manager. Tooling differs, and <a href="/languages">the reference chapter</a> lists
    the exact tool per language for each enforcement step.
  </p>

  <Note label="Before you start" variant="warn">
    <p>
      Do not apply all nine rules to an existing codebase in one week. Pick one
      module, apply them there, and let the diff be the argument. OTAKU spreads by
      example, not by decree.
    </p>
  </Note>
